## Why this one

I started using my own version of the “Pod” in 2017 while living out of a 70-liter Hyperlite Porter during five months of travel in New Zealand. The pack was essentially one large compartment, and the “pods” gave me a way to divide it without adding much weight or bulk. I could keep clothing, sleep gear, and smaller items together while still packing the bag as a simple vertical stack.

Now, HMG makes a set and I mostly use them because that approach works in more than one pack. They add just enough organization to a minimalist bag without dictating how everything else has to fit.

## In use

The Pods have held up through years of packing and unpacking. Their semi-transparent Dyneema Composite Fabric lets me identify what’s inside at a glance (while maintaining a little mystery), and the fabric doesn’t absorb water or stretch out of shape. The zipper and taped seams provide some water resistance, though I don’t treat them as dry bags.

They work particularly well in my Arc’teryx SL 30, which I use for travel lasting a week or more. I also use them in duffels and at home. They live in my closet holding small pouches/accessories, then come along when I travel.
## What I’d change
In general, I am vehemently anti-tiny zipper, especially waterproof versions. If I could think of a way to design them out of the product, I would. Since I can’t I treat them with care and. pack to the available volume, no more. 
